<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.3.2: Recognizing Pairs of Solutions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02124</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.3.2: Recognizing Pairs of Solutions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>b6dc89fc-ab4f-4c81-bacb-62eb7d7601f3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>A whole number that has two factors that are equal to each other is called a perfect square. Those equal factors that multiply to be the number are called the <term class="glossary-tooltip">square root of a number</term>. For instance, \(3\) is a square root of \(9\) because \(3 \cdot 3 =
  9.\).</para>

<note>
  <para>SQUARE ROOT PROPERTY</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(x^2 = k\), then</para>
  <para>\(x=\sqrt{k} \quad \text { and } \quad x=-\sqrt{k} \rightarrow x=\pm \sqrt{k}\).</para>
</note>

<para>Each of these equations has two solutions. Use your knowledge of square roots and the square root property. What are the solutions? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(n^2 + 4=404\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> \(n=20\) and \(n=-20\)</para>
    
    <para>If \(n^2 + 4 = 404\), then \(n^2 = 400\).
      Using the square root property, \(n= \pm \sqrt{400}\).
      Therefore, \(n=20\) and \(n=-20\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(432=3n^2\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(n=12\) and \(n=-12\)</para>
    
    <para>Divide each side of the equation by 3.
      \(n^2=144\)
      Using the square root property, \(n= \pm \sqrt{144}\).
      Therefore, \(n=12\) and \(n=-12\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(\frac{n^2}{5}−20=0\)</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para> \(n=10\) and \(n=-10\)</para>
    
    <para>Add 20 to each side of the equation.
      Then multiply each side of the equation by 5.
      \(n^2 = 100\)
      Using the square root property, \(n= \pm \sqrt{100}\).
      Therefore, \(n=10\) and \(n=-10\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(2n^2−7=65\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(n=6\) and \(n=-6\)</para>
    
    <para>Add 7 to each side of the equation.
      Divide each side of the equation by 2.
      \(n^2=36\)
      Using the square root property, \(n= \pm \sqrt{36}\).
      Therefore, \(n=6\) and \(n=-6\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

